import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   What a `String` is on JavaScript.
   ==========================================================================================

   `String.yana` has the contract and every operation; this file has the two records the operations
   are *about*. `compiler/resolve`, the stage J173 deleted, registered a JS string as a primitive
   (`Type::String`), so the library declared one for native only and the JavaScript half of
   `String.yana` was written against a type nothing in `lib/` names. The compiler holds no name for
   a string at all - it reads `@builtin(string)` - so on this target it had none, which was
   milestone 0's wall 1 and the reason `Core` did not resolve for JavaScript
   (`doc/progress/2026-09-21-J63-jsir-milestone-0.md` §3).

   **The library declares it and the compiler registers nothing**, which is the decision rather than
   the shortest path. Registering a second primitive would have put the target back inside the
   resolver - a type whose fields, ownership and view the compiler knows by name - which is exactly
   what `@builtin(string)` replaced on the other target. A record claiming the role is read by the
   same three questions the native one is read by, and the target-specific part of the answer is one
   line in JSIR rather than a kind in `type.def`.

   **`.js.yana`**, because a native build reads `String.yana`'s own `@platform(native)` records.
-}

{-
   The owner - one opaque handle on the host's own `string`.

   **`%U8` is the library's existing spelling for a host value nothing reads through**, and
   `Unicode/Collator.js.yana`'s `Collator {host: %U8}` is the precedent: the pointee type carries no
   meaning, and what makes it right *here* is precisely that a host string has no addressable units,
   so there is nothing the pointee could honestly be. Nothing in `lib/` reads this field - every
   operation in `String.yana`'s JavaScript half takes `self: String` and hands the whole value to a
   host role, which is what lets JSIR map the record to the single `HostString` value
   `Analysis-IR-JS.md` §3.2.2 decides on. A field the library read would be a property access on a
   primitive.

   A newtype over `%U8` rather than a bare `%U8` alias, because the role wants a *record* -
   `known.def`'s `string` row is `Entity::Record` - and because a bare pointer would make
   `String` arithmetic-compatible with a host array, which nothing wants.
-}
@builtin(string)
pub data String {host: %U8}

{-
   **There is deliberately no `StringView` here, and no `Viewed(String)`.**

   That is Analysis-IR-JS.md §3.2.2 said in the library: on this target a shared `String` *is* the
   string. The native view is two words into the owner's units, because a native slice is a window
   and a window is worth carrying; a host string has no units to point into, so a slice is
   `String.prototype.slice` - the engine's own copy - and a window here would mean carrying
   `(string, from, to)` at every parameter, field and block argument in the program to save a copy at
   the few places that cut.

   So the `stringView` row is unclaimed on this target and the view row for `String` is not live at
   all - `viewRowOf` asks for both halves, and `known.def`'s row is `Optional` for this reason. What
   that buys beyond the representation is that the JavaScript half of `String.yana` says exactly what
   it looks like it says: `hostCall1(self, "charCodeAt", index)` hands over the string, not a
   derivation of it, and the member literal reaches the emitter as the constant it was written as.

   A one-field record with no teardown is trivially copyable, which is the right answer here and not
   a coincidence: the collector owns every host string, `Reclaim(String)` has no instance on this
   target, and two names for one host string cannot observe each other.
-}

{-
   `String.slice` on JavaScript - the **window signature** over a copying body.

   `String.yana` has the contract and `String.native.yana` the other target's body. Every caller in
   `lib/` - `Text.yana`'s trims, splits and searches are all of them - is written
   against the window, so this has to declare one: `text: source'String` in and `source'String` out,
   rooted in the string it cuts from. What comes back is the host's own copy rather than a window
   into `text`, and Analysis-IR-JS.md §3.2.2 is the argument that the difference is unobservable -
   a copy aliases *less* than a window, and the only thing a program could see is a write through the
   owner while a window into it is live, which the loan checker refuses on both targets.

   The clamping is the native body's, in the same order, because the two have to answer the same
   thing and the host's own clamping is not that order.
-}
pub fn String.slice(text: source'String, from: Size, to: Size) -> source'String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &start = from
    if start < 0 then start = 0
    if start > count then start = count

    let &end = to
    if end < start then end = start
    if end > count then end = count

    return hostSubstring(text, start, end)
